---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import { firstComponentButtonsTree } from "../../demos/button";
import { getLocale, useTranslations } from "../../i18n";

const step1 = `pnpm add @skryensya/core`;

const step2 = `@import "@skryensya/core/tokens.scss";`;

const step3 = `@import "@skryensya/core/tokens.scss";
@import "@skryensya/core/components/button.css";`;

const step4 = `/* A hook, not a new selector: all variants
   they read the same variable, oh my, this restyles them
   all within .hero. */
.hero .sk-button {
  --sk-button-bg: rebeccapurple;
  --sk-button-radius: var(--radius-pill);
}`;

const t = useTranslations(getLocale(Astro.url));

const step6html = `<button
  class="sk-button sk-interactive"
  data-sk-button
  data-variant="primary"
>
  Save
</button>`;

/* The only place where you touch localStorage by hand, because it runs before any module and can't
   wait for one. The key and the slot still come out of the declaration; see /storage. */
const fouc = `<script>
  // Before the first paint, synchronous: it can't import,
  // and it's the only exception in the system (see Storage).
  var s = {};
  try { s = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('sk') || '{}'); } catch (e) {}
  if (s.scheme) document.documentElement.style.colorScheme = s.scheme;
</script>`;
---

<Base
  title="Your first component"
  description="From zero to a button that responds to the four dimensions, step by step."
>
  <h1>Your first component</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    From zero to a button that responds to mode, contrast, density and radius. Six steps, no magic.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={step1} label="terminal" />

  <h2>2. Import the base bundle</h2>
  <p>
    It is the only thing that every consumer needs: primitives + semantics. With this you already have the four dimensions and the language of motion.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={step2} label="CSS" />
  <p>
    The bare specifiers are resolved by the <code>exports</code> map, so you need a bundler (Vite, webpack, Parcel).
  </p>

  <h2>3. Import the component you are going to use</h2>
  <p>Only what you send. A buttonless consumer never downloads this file.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={step3} label="CSS" />
  <p>
    With that you now have a button. <code>button.css</code> brings the <em>class and</em> its <a href="/en/styling-hooks">styling hooks</a>: there is no step where you have to write the structural CSS for something to be seen. The order of the imports does not matter, the <a href="/en/tiers">cascade layers</a> decide the precedence.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Uselo</h2>
  <p>
    Variants and sizes are attributes. You do not need new classes or branching in your CSS: all <a href="/en/styling-hooks">variants re-declare the same</a> styling hook.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={firstComponentButtonsTree(t)} label="Button" />

  <h2>5. Change it</h2>
  <p>
    Sending the structure does not leave you outside of it: that is what hooks are for. The structure lives in <code>@layer components</code> and <strong>any no-layer rule beats any</strong> layer, so you re-declare a hook from your CSS and win, without inventing a more specific selector or fighting with <code>!important</code>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={step4} label="CSS" note="opcional" />

  <h2>6. Interactive states</h2>
  <p>
    Hover, focus and pressed are not button colors: they are provided by the <a href="/en/state-layer">state layer</a>, an overlay of <code>currentColor</code> shared by every interactive component. It is the system interaction mechanism and comes in <code>tokens.scss</code>, there is nothing to import separately. Just add <code>sk-interactive</code>: zero state colors to your CSS, and the focus ring is included.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={step6html} label="HTML" note="one more class" />

  <h2>What's missing: the FOUC guard</h2>
  <p>
    If users can choose the mode, apply it <strong>before the first paint</strong>, synchronously in
    the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>. Otherwise, the page first paints without the selected brand and mode, then jumps.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={fouc} label="HTML, in the head" />

  <div class="sk-callout" data-tone="info">
    <div class="sk-callout__content">
    <p>
      <strong>That is the entire contract.</strong> Two imports and one class produce a button that
      responds to two brands, light and dark modes, high contrast, and three densities, with the
      WCAG minimum target size guaranteed by the token. None of that appears in your code, and
      neither does a line of structural CSS.
    </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
